plan.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/flow.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/flow_graph.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/flow_tests.rs","wave/infrastructure/MEMORY.md"]},{"sha":"16fa9742591e3edfc0ed42c913c64347c02ffeb5","title":"Resume the current worktree’s last conversation with lf resume (#1430)","files":["README.md","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/session.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/util.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/navigation.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/human_session.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/human_session/provider_conversation.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sessions.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/sessions.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/session_cli_tests.rs"]},{"sha":"7a6ae0d7094f97ca5a8895e21abc466e557e7ab5","title":"Speed up lf wt list and report real invocation timings (#1427)","files":[".lf/flows/pursue-auto.yaml","docs/lf-reference.md","docs/lf.md","docs/troubleshooting.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/worktrees.rs","rust/loopflow/src/harness/codex.rs","rust/loopflow/src/harness/codex_connection.rs","rust/loopflow/src/harness/dispatch.rs","rust/loopflow/src/harness/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/journal/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/ops/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task/lifecycle.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task_automation.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/wt.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/wt_timing.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/worktree_tests.rs","scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/20261004/baseline-recheck.jsonl","scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/20261004/baseline.jsonl","scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/20261004/candidate-offline.jsonl","scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/20261004/candidate.jsonl","scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/README.md","scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/profile.py","wave/infrastructure/MEMORY.md"]},{"sha":"464ac18f0100a2fee0504e1f0684b8714e104088","title":"Record and report real Desktop launch and refresh timings (#1432)","files":["python/tests/test_desktop_timings.py","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/README.md","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/timings.py","scripts/test_desktop.sh","swift/LoopflowMac/LoopflowApp.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/PodiumModel.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/LaunchJournal.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/Perf.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/RegistryQueryLocal.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/PodiumView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkspaceNavigator.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/WorkspaceCache.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/LaunchJournalTests.swift","swift/README.md","wave/product/MEMORY.md"]},{"sha":"58d3b5b3e05135a3267d0f5340e435044440bb49","title":"Record installation and checkout closure evidence for LOO-292 (#1433)","files":["wave/infrastructure/MEMORY.md"]}],"merged_prs":[{"number":1396,"title":"build(deps-dev): bump ruff from 0.16.4 to 0.16.9 in the python group","body":"Bumps the python group with 1 update: [ruff](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff).\n\nUpdates `ruff` from 0.16.4 to 0.16.9\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/releases\">ruff's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.16.9</h2>\n<h2>Release Notes</h2>\n<p>Released on 2026-09-24.</p>\n<h3>Preview features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>ruff</code>] Avoid false positives for overloaded division (<code>RUF069</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28309\">#28309</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Bug fixes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>flake8-bugbear</code>] Avoid false positives for calls with keyword arguments (<code>B009</code>, <code>B010</code>, <code>B043</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28776\">#28776</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>flake8-tidy-imports</code>] Allow lazy imports to be used in deferred annotations (<code>TID255</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28767\">#28767</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Rule changes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Update LibCST-based fixes for Python 3.15 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28616\">#28616</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>flake8-pyi</code>] Mention stubs in the diagnostic message (<code>PYI002</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28542\">#28542</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Documentation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Fix horizontal overflow on the rules documentation page (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28699\">#28699</a>)</li>\n<li>Update rules table with category information (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28651\">#28651</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>flake8-annotations</code>] Clarify that <code>ANN401</code> checks return types in addition to arguments (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28334\">#28334</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>flake8-bugbear</code>] Document type-checker interaction (<code>B010</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28509\">#28509</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>flake8-comprehensions</code>] Document <code>map</code>/generator exception behavior (<code>C417</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27794\">#27794</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>ruff</code>] Mention related isort settings (<code>RUF022</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28719\">#28719</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Contributors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/qinpei-dev\"><code>@​qinpei-dev</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/sanjayrohith\"><code>@​sanjayrohith</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/ntBre\"><code>@​ntBre</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/webdevsamran\"><code>@​webdevsamran</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/zaniebot\"><code>@​zaniebot</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/ewdurbin\"><code>@​ewdurbin</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/MichaReiser\"><code>@​MichaReiser</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/spaceone\"><code>@​spaceone</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/IbrahimKhan12\"><code>@​IbrahimKhan12</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/devtechedge\"><code>@​devtechedge</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/GruffElixir\"><code>@​GruffElixir</code></a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Install ruff 0.16.9</h2>\n<h3>Install prebuilt binaries via shell script</h3>\n<pre lang=\"sh\"><code>curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -LsSf https://releases.astral.sh/github/ruff/releases/download/0.16.9/ruff-installer.sh | sh\n</code></pre>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\">ruff's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.16.9</h2>\n<p>Released on 2026-09-24.</p>\n<h3>Preview features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>ruff</code>] Avoid false positives for overloaded division (<code>RUF069</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/28309\">#28309</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Bug fixes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>flake8-bugbear","files":[],"additions":69,"deletions":69,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"2c2a0ae496458842fe15d5e873120c8c0ab8ed8d"},{"number":1397,"title":"build(deps): bump the rust group with 2 updates","body":"Bumps the rust group with 2 updates: [thiserror](https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror) and [tiktoken-rs](https://github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs).\n\nUpdates `thiserror` from 2.0.20 to 2.0.21\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/releases\">thiserror's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>2.0.21</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fix parsing of generic unit variants in display expressions (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/issues/459\">#459</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/b1827ee06f81a7d7f954e676771e19a97f9f8e3b\"><code>b1827ee</code></a> Release 2.0.21</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/58037b575a2a5543d0d54a49360554325b49f55a\"><code>58037b5</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/issues/459\">#459</a> from dtolnay/turbofish</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/f82a0cf8f06c71263c2caa26e759f337d2778153\"><code>f82a0cf</code></a> Keep track of nested turbofish depth</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/72ea49262d6412ccbc08f1696dda8626409e657d\"><code>72ea492</code></a> Raise required compiler to Rust 1.77</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/72eea0d4ddb17ff9fa873aa74469de850021b22e\"><code>72eea0d</code></a> Resolve io_other_error clippy lint in tests</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/07f09a2ec934df58508ce4fa5e7fbf27cca552c2\"><code>07f09a2</code></a> Raise required compiler to Rust 1.74</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/2715388e8cc5787b3643251f7a7a637e89376688\"><code>2715388</code></a> Update ui test suite to nightly-2026-09-22</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/5a306c7d0a8588caaaaa6a7567aeb25c1c10719b\"><code>5a306c7</code></a> Update ui test suite to nightly-2026-09-05</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/ef9383b37d7c96b01e10df35d3e9e313c7b98b5a\"><code>ef9383b</code></a> Update ui test suite to nightly-2026-08-22</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/commit/8336b8407fbfe69177c504cdbc379db20cf6f131\"><code>8336b84</code></a> Update ui tests for version 2.0.20</li>\n<li>See full diff in <a href=\"https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror/compare/2.0.20...2.0.21\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\nUpdates `tiktoken-rs` from 0.12.0 to 0.12.1\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs/releases\">tiktoken-rs's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v0.12.1</h2>\n<p>A maintenance release that refreshes upstream tiktoken and restores compatibility with the declared Rust 1.85 minimum. Existing public function signatures are unchanged.</p>\n<h2>Changes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Refresh the vendor snapshot to OpenAI tiktoken 0.14.0 and align regex dependencies. GPT-5 tokenizer lookup follows upstream's family-prefix matching. (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs/issues/173\">#173</a>)</li>\n<li>Use a 1,050,000-token context-window default for <code>gpt-6*</code> names. This adds context metadata only; GPT-6 tokenizer lookup and token-budget helpers remain unsupported. (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs/issues/174\">#174</a>)</li>\n<li>Fix Rust 1.85 compilation and the default-feature README doctest; test both default and all features across supported platforms. (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs/issues/170\">#170</a>)</li>\n<li>Replace <code>lazy_static</code> with the standard library's <code>LazyLock</code>. (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs/issues/165\">#165</a>)</li>\n<li>Clarify README instructions and remove release-specific wording. (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs/issues/172\">#172</a>)</li>\n<li>Fail release detection on registry errors and remove inactive upload jobs. (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zurawiki/tikt","files":[],"additions":18,"deletions":30,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"af3254515fa8036414e0059c28bdc34e73267419"},{"number":1398,"title":"build(deps): bump tokio-tungstenite from 0.29.0 to 0.30.0","body":"Bumps [tokio-tungstenite](https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite) from 0.29.0 to 0.30.0.\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md\">tokio-tungstenite's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h1>0.30.0</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Update <code>tungstenite</code> to <code>0.30.0</code>. See <a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tungstenite-rs/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md\"><code>tungstenite</code> release</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/commit/4994a078037a18a9347b8cf49291a5031d63d9e0\"><code>4994a07</code></a> Bump version</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/commit/753ca72690919485a1aa1f0f69a336b1152fb0ae\"><code>753ca72</code></a> Document cancel safety of reading from WebSocketStream (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/issues/378\">#378</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/commit/751d7e2bc26e5de302f4a79907b6949bf00e0043\"><code>751d7e2</code></a> Update version number listed in Readme (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/issues/375\">#375</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/commit/57fc3d0276564efaf11efe8c1499b26b44dbe9a4\"><code>57fc3d0</code></a> docs(CHANGELOG.md): fix <code>tungstenite</code> versions (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/issues/374\">#374</a>)</li>\n<li>See full diff in <a href=\"https://github.com/snapview/tokio-tungstenite/compare/v0.29.0...v0.30.0\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=tokio-tungstenite&package-manager=cargo&previous-version=0.29.0&new-version=0.30.0)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>","files":[],"additions":60,"deletions":16,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"b7ed4784a6789ef777d6f5f20129828c8c53123e"},{"number":1399,"title":"build(deps): bump dirs from 6.0.0 to 7.0.0","body":"Bumps dirs from 6.0.0 to 7.0.0.\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=dirs&package-manager=cargo&previous-version=6.0.0&new-version=7.0.0)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>","files":[],"additions":3,"deletions":3,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"cd344891b611914adf44eeb53844193d7a987d2c"},{"number":1412,"title":"Keep Wave and repository workspaces persistent with local scratch","body":"Wave and repository conversations previously lacked a durable workspace for maintaining and publishing documents independently of Tasks. They now reuse dedicated worktrees across conversation replacement, keeping local plans intact through publication and landing.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Give repository and Wave primary conversations separate resident workspaces without Task membership. Reuse moved checkouts, recover committed state from missing checkouts, and protect resident worktrees from automatic pruning.\n- Add `lf wt create <name> --resident` and selected-path commits: `lf commit -m \"Record decisions\" <document-path>` preserves unrelated staged and unstaged edits.\n- Keep resident scratch on disk while removing it from the published tree. Resident publication sends the committed range without automatically committing later local edits; non-resident landing still clears scratch.\n- Preserve both original notes and resolver-created files when sync encounters untracked filename collisions.\n- Move `pr`, `wt`, `sync`, and `commit` out of the Task command hierarchy. Retain automatic shortcuts, update callers and documentation, and migrate saved Flow commands without changing their identity or progress.\n\nMaintenance remains explicit through `lf sync`; scheduled document distribution is outside this change. Removing tracked scratch does not erase copies in earlier commits.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch checks passed for focused workspace, commit, publication, landing, sync, CLI, and Flow behavior, plus formatting and Clippy. The broader library run was interrupted after chapter/migration failures; no full-suite pass is claimed. One installation-only Session case remains deferred to Linux CI.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough; the full interactive demo remains incomplete:\n\nCreate a workspace with `lf wt create planning --resident` and enter its printed path. Edit a document, add a note under `scratch/`, and stage an unrelated file. Commit only the document with `lf commit -m \"Record decisions\" <document-path>`, then inspect the committed range before running `lf pr publish`.\n\nThe document should appear in the PR while scratch stays local and the unrelated file remains staged. After merging, run `lf sync`: the same workspace and local notes should remain available.","files":[],"additions":2313,"deletions":936,"changed_files":127,"merge_commit":"10442e871a061ed8f883aaae3c7aceeae51eaa56"},{"number":1413,"title":"Recover transient release reads and restart Tasks without Linear","body":"A transient GitHub read no longer forces Jack to rerun release preparation, and existing Tasks can restart or continue when Linear is unreachable. Tests run with external networking denied so accidental service dependencies fail locally instead of stalling CI.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Make the release and CI paths survive a hang, a dropped connection and a slow Linear · LOO-326](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-326/make-the-release-and-ci-paths-survive-a-hang-a-dropped-connection-and)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Retry artifact downloads and individual check pages up to three times, with bounded process and pipe cleanup. Each download attempt gets a fresh directory; candidate identity, publisher writes and exact-head checks remain unchanged.\n- Validate existing Task admission against cached planning at restart, continuation and every managed execution boundary. Known invalidation, removal, terminal state and ownership mismatches still block. New advice must publish before replacement.\n- Separate dependency/build setup from isolated Rust, Python, Swift, website, smoke and installation-container test execution. Probe both external denial and usable loopback. Preserve the shipped Swift cancellation and telemetry fixture repairs.\n- Reconcile infrastructure memory within its context budget, retaining dated evidence in Git history.\n\n## Checks\n\nFocused local proofs passed for read deadlines/retry exhaustion, partial-artifact recovery, public restart through replacement execution and six preservation scenarios, and all 37 PR-reader tests. Isolated Python repair checks and final command-plan assertions passed; formatting and Ruff passed.\n\nHosted Rust, Clippy, Python, website, smoke, installation-container, architecture, migration and app-build checks passed. Swift initially rejected nested sandboxes before test execution; the corrected wrapper combines the rules in one sandbox, with local denial/loopback/ps probes passing. The prior published head passed the full hosted matrix; this repaired head requires fresh CI.\n\nThe full local gate stopped before product tests because free disk fell below the repository's 32 GiB reserve. Local proofs used disposable repositories/providers; no live release or installation was changed. macOS retains Desktop's existing exception for the fixed system ps reader.\n\n## Independent review repair\n\nJack Heart requested an independent review, then authorized holding, fixing and landing.\nRestart now reports that replacement stopped while retaining the confirmed or\nuncertain Linear publication result. It no longer claims accepted advice was\nnot published. Regression coverage exercises accepted writes followed by failed\nobservation, uncertain writes/readback, and unchanged execution on failed restart.\nThe separate nonblocking diagnostic finding about generic permanent read errors\nremains a limitation; no release or installed acceptance is claimed.","files":[],"additions":870,"deletions":81,"changed_files":23,"merge_commit":"acd6654f99b02d7f34238067c0f0ccfbbfd9a20a"},{"number":1414,"title":"Clarify published installation and weekly schedule evidence","body":"Outdated refresh requirements and installation diagnoses made it difficult to tell what had been verified on the laptop. This documentation-only PR reconciles the evidence with the current command responsibilities and makes the remaining verification work explicit.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Keep published installation and main checkout updates reliable · LOO-292](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-292/keep-published-installation-and-main-checkout-updates-reliable)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 2 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Clarify that `lf install` updates published machine artifacts, while `lf rebase` owns checkout updates; scheduling defaults to opt-in login plus weekly execution.\n- Record published 0.12.31 preflight, executable hash and app signature checks, alongside operator evidence of repeat installation, repeatable schedule activation and initial launchd success.\n- Mark obsolete publication blockers and the mistaken missing-daemon diagnosis as historical, and preserve lasting decisions in infrastructure Wave memory.\n- Retain outstanding proof for fresh installation and repair, history preservation, installed checkout updates, retained-candidate preflight, natural scheduled or wake execution, and interactive app acceptance. Initial job success does not establish these outcomes.\n\n## Checks\n\n`git diff --check main...HEAD` passes. Historical test results are labeled as such. The recorded validation runner timed out during resource measurement before architecture checks ran; those checks remain deferred to CI.\n\nJack Heart approved the review and explicitly requested autonomous landing on October 4. This documentation PR keeps LOO-292 open for the listed operator proof; no additional installation or schedule outcome is claimed.","files":[],"additions":21,"deletions":0,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"2115a8ad42aaa3a952d485175a7018e6dd49773a"},{"number":1416,"title":"Share Claude and Codex conversations between Loopflow and their native CLIs","body":"Loopflow previously kept conversations in separate account homes, making them harder to find and resume from plain Claude or Codex. Conversations now use the provider’s ordinary home by default, with stored account switching and explicit isolation when needed.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf account <provider> use <email>` to switch the shared login while preserving refreshed or previously unknown credentials. Routing keeps an eligible active account and selects another when needed.\n- Add `--isolate`, `--shared`, and the `isolate` configuration default. Resuming preserves each conversation’s original home mode; existing account-pinned conversations remain isolated.\n- Accept native conversation IDs in `lf session` commands and import previously untracked conversations when connecting.\n- Close owned Codex engines on driver exit while preserving saved history, live takeovers, and unrelated conversations. Retry headless turns interrupted by a shared account switch.\n- Forward SSH credentials without writing logins to the target’s disk; Codex receives its token through the engine protocol.\n- Refocus generated PR reviews on behavior, data models, and APIs.\n\n## Limitations\n\nSwitching accounts changes the login used by plain Claude and Codex too. Running Codex engines retain their original login until restarted; an in-flight turn may fail once, with automatic recovery for headless runs. Shared Codex switching requires file-based credential storage. Forwarded Codex login uses an experimental protocol and requires Loopflow’s engine.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdds coverage for credential preservation, shared and isolated routing, native conversation IDs, and engine ownership, plus provider fixtures using synthetic credentials and local endpoints. Tests were not run while preparing this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed for this description: start a conversation with `lf -m codex : \"say hi\"`, finish it, then run `codex resume`. The conversation should appear in Codex’s normal history. Switch to another stored login with `lf account codex use <email>` and inspect `lf account route`; the new account should be active while the saved conversation remains available.","files":[],"additions":3968,"deletions":436,"changed_files":49,"merge_commit":"4bff3b7f7515d4d6344a126ee22c8bbb6c8177d9"},{"number":1418,"title":"Choose a New Session skill and capture Tasks without moving existing work","body":"Desktop had no dedicated entry point for turning ideas into Tasks while keeping existing work in place. New Session now launches a chosen skill, defaults to `capture-tasks`, and remembers the choice per repository.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Capture new Tasks from Desktop conversations · LOO-368](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-368/capture-new-tasks-from-desktop-conversations)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add a searchable skill picker beside New Session, using repository-local discovery. Choosing a skill updates the next launch without starting it.\n- Launch from the repository sidebar or a Wave’s context menu. A selected Task supplies its parent Wave; the new Session opens in the repository checkout while preserving Task selection and existing terminal layouts.\n- Add `capture-tasks` guidance for exploring ideas, resolving ownership across Waves and repositories, and filing self-contained Tasks without starting workers or binding the capture conversation. Existing Wave conversations reuse this guidance.\n- Preserve successful filings and reconcile uncertain writes before retrying identical input in the destination repository.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch checks include six passing TaskCaptureTests after integration with main, covering discovery, remembered selection, launch scope, layout preservation, and helper-resolution failures. Earlier builtin tests, navigation tests, Swift build, formatting, and Clippy also passed. The October 4 synced build/sign/open and all six TaskCaptureTests also passed.\n\nNative picker interaction, live provider continuation, draft preservation, and cross-repository filing remain unverified. The configured CLI must include both `capture-tasks` and explicit skill launch support; an earlier demo showed that building the app alone did not supply the required runtime.\n\nJack Heart approved the demo on October 4 and explicitly requested landing. Individual scenario results above remain unverified; approval records the review decision without inventing those observations.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not yet verified end to end: with a Task selected and existing terminals open, choose `capture-tasks` beside New Session, then click New Session. A repository conversation should open with the Task’s parent Wave as context, retaining the existing panes and Task selection. Describe an idea, capture its Tasks, then continue with another idea in the same conversation.","files":[],"additions":596,"deletions":36,"changed_files":13,"merge_commit":"b1f79c5f75fa0f3cde7a3980ecae5db09d8182bd"},{"number":1419,"title":"Track scheduled releases through verified publication and interruption recovery","body":"A successful cron exit previously could not show whether a release reached users or what happened to missed firings. This PR connects each scheduled release opportunity to its execution, verification and product outcome, preserving unfinished candidates and failure evidence through retries.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Account for scheduled release opportunities and settle actual release outcomes · LOO-285](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-285/account-for-scheduled-release-opportunities-and-settle-actual-release)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf release history` to distinguish verified publication, verified no-change, deferred work and failures, with repair ownership recorded through `lf cron disposition`.\n- Catch up outstanding due times with one execution per wake. Preserve original timing, candidate ownership and operator interventions; collapsed misses cannot count as additional successful releases.\n- Continue unfinished candidates across schedule replacements on the same Home. Home changes retain unresolved work for repair without transferring execution authority.\n- Retry missing or failed current telemetry once per wake. Interrupted checks require process and lock evidence before recovery; bounded waits defer release without killing surviving checks or inventing results.\n- Verify the exact candidate and public artifacts before settlement. Reconcile missing publication stages from retained artifacts, require public read-back and installer smoke, and preserve newer releases and conflicting immutable assets.\n- Carry release locks and checkout leases through Git, PR, hooks, CI repair and publisher children so controller death cannot permit overlapping publication or premature checkout removal.\n\n## Checks\n\nPassed: 67 release integration tests, 46 focused cron tests, 30 publisher tests, process tests (9 passed; isolated tmux test ignored), formatting and all-target Clippy. All 11 scheduled-release scenarios passed. Interruption/lock coverage passed across 14 suite cases plus the repaired auto-merge scenario run separately; simulated providers do not establish production release success.\n\nJack Heart waived the interactive demo and requested landing. Installed acceptance, public artifact/installer proof and two distinct automatic executions with at least one publication remain follow-up work; this PR does not request Task completion.\n\nReview repairs preserve main's explicit detached-child cwd and update stale fixtures to use current PR observations, explicit missing-release responses, and published source commits.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough after installation: run `lf release history --wave infrastructure --days 35 --json`. Inspect an opportunity's original due time, linked attempts, verification and outcome. Missed dues covered by one execution should retain their identities while contributing only one settlement; unresolved failures should remain visible after recovery.","files":[],"additions":11163,"deletions":708,"changed_files":48,"merge_commit":"25dbcde69e4cad5fe1f04aee488fc562aa4e958e"},{"number":1421,"title":"Show unfinished interactive Sessions in ordinary navigation","body":"Background agent conversations cluttered Task navigation. Ordinary Desktop and CLI Session lists now show unfinished interactive conversations and current authored reviews, with matching navigation counts.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Show only unfinished interactive Sessions in Task navigation · LOO-372](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-372/show-only-unfinished-interactive-sessions-in-task-navigation)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Desktop uses the shared interactive default; `--needs-me` narrows the selected mode.\n- Filtered absence preserves selection, prepared commands, drafts and retained native surfaces. Explicit completion remains separate from a finished turn.\n- Show headless Sessions and `lf session list --interactive all --history` retain full inspection. Task association and review authority are unchanged.\n\n## Checks\n\nThe Desktop build and 47 focused headless tests passed after compression; an earlier broader run passed 92. API inventory and review-completion/resume integration tests passed, along with 12 Session-event tests, formatting and all-target Clippy. Gate reused unchanged-code receipts; architecture and Swift boundary checks passed. CI owns the full matrix.\n\nProvider review coverage uses a stand-in. These checks do not establish configured provider input, native visual acceptance, sustained use or latency improvements. Direct-opening and performance work remain separate in LOO-371 and LOO-304.\n\n## Try it\n\nOpen a Task containing interactive and background conversations: its ordinary Session list and counts should include only unfinished interactive participation. Enable Show headless Sessions to inspect background work. This is a suggested walkthrough; headless regressions cover the behavior.","files":[],"additions":218,"deletions":112,"changed_files":16,"merge_commit":"4cb891ee8d1f31ca99b029a55806df252e24eb2a"},{"number":1422,"title":"Recover retained PR landings and start workers after checkout removal","body":"Obsolete Home supervision could prevent pending PR landings from loading, while a tmux server left in a deleted checkout could prevent detached workers from starting. This PR preserves retained deliveries through migration and explicitly starts workers in their requested checkout.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Archive retired Home supervisor identity, PID, heartbeat and generation on each landing; advance the generation and clear active supervision while preserving delivery intent, failures, CI state and terminal history.\n- Reject stale supervisor writes and prevent older executables from acquiring fresh Home claims. Existing local supervision remains intact.\n- Set the tmux client’s working directory and enter the shell-quoted checkout inside the detached child before execution. Spawn errors include the requested directory.\n- Curate infrastructure memory and preserve release recovery evidence and historical decisions through Git links.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch evidence reports passing focused landing and process tests, including migration/read/reconciliation coverage and an isolated tmux test with a deleted server directory. All-target Clippy and the migration catalog check also passed. These checks were not rerun while preparing this description.\n\nThe reconciliation regression uses simulated provider observations. Installed acceptance still requires a published patch; the separately recovered v0.13.0 release predates these fixes. The tmux reproduction establishes the deleted-directory defect, but does not establish the cause of every reported worker timeout.","files":[],"additions":652,"deletions":917,"changed_files":6,"merge_commit":"52ab4a4a5cf1ec3c24b019d5cee3a1c782a30d9b"},{"number":1423,"title":"Enqueue passing Dependabot updates with a dedicated merge token","body":"Dependabot PRs enabled auto-merge but never entered the required merge queue because the workflow used GITHUB_TOKEN. Use the dedicated repository token, retry after successful CI, and explicitly enqueue the unchanged head when queue membership is absent. Keep weekly grouping and all required checks. The secret is installed, and the token successfully enqueued the four existing updates. YAML parsing, shell syntax, and whitespace checks passed; hosted CI verifies the published workflow.","files":[],"additions":42,"deletions":3,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"2d0d5cb91ac4bf406e941f0991b8ee974b83ebd8"},{"number":1424,"title":"Prevent Session dispatch deadlocks and confirm Linear writes without Wave sweeps","body":"A stalled Codex dispatch could hold SQLite’s writer transaction while the current-thread event loop tried to record history through the same connection mutex. The event loop also owned the dispatch timeout, so one launch could freeze the shared Home. Native dispatch now uses a Session-scoped OS lock shared with driver claim, release, and exit; SQLite is released before provider I/O. Both Codex dispatch paths and OpenCode use this boundary, preserving stale-driver rejection without blocking unrelated database writes.\n\nETU-89 exposed a separate failure: Linear committed issue creation, then a five-second refresh of the entire Wave failed and prevented local preparation. Creation and edit/completion now confirm the exact issue, reconcile its Project association, and retain duplicate-safe retries. Issue confirmation does not claim fresh observation of the whole Wave.\n\nValidation: 31 affected Rust tests; current-thread regression with a real backpressured WebSocket, SQLite history, independent writer, driver transfer, and an external process watchdog; stateful Linear creation/edit/confirmation recovery tests; cargo fmt, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, and architecture checks.\n\nReview preserved driver exclusion on every ownership change and exact Project reconciliation. Lock acquisition uses a bounded OS-clock wait; existing provider transport deadlines remain in force. Existing running binaries retain their old behavior until upgraded. No live-store migration or issue recreation is involved.\n","files":[],"additions":349,"deletions":48,"changed_files":9,"merge_commit":"cb4dd53c94866c7f6e30736977e29e800e9e4c67"},{"number":1425,"title":"Open Desktop on the saved workspace while fresh data loads","body":"Reopening Desktop previously waited for slow planning and Session reads before showing useful work. Returning launches now restore the saved workspace and selected Wave or Task immediately, while fresh data loads in the background.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Open Desktop immediately from one cached workspace · LOO-376](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-376/open-desktop-immediately-from-one-cached-workspace)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Share one Home-scoped workspace cache across windows, including Portfolio, with one refresh owner per window.\n- Replace separate loading and refresh-error messages with one workspace status. Failed refreshes retain the last available content.\n- Clear saved Flow execution, Task condition and Session state; controls wait for fresh data, while opening a Session remains validated by `lf`.\n- Discard incompatible caches and content from a different Home. Retain up to eight repositories.\n\nFirst launches still wait for reads. Open conversations are not automatically restored. Production launch timing and rendered startup validation remain follow-up work.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Recorded validation: 14 cache tests passed after merging `main`; the earlier 124-test workspace run is documented as applicable to unchanged code. Full-suite and Xcode app-build checks remain deferred to gate.\n- A 20-sample simulated replay measured saved workspace restoration at 9.6 ms median / 9.8 ms p95 versus 13.2 s uncached. These measure model content availability, not rendered startup; fresh data still took about 13.2 s. See the [benchmark evidence](scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/20261004-startup-inprocess/README.md).\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed in a rendered app: open Desktop, let the workspace load, select a Task, then quit and reopen. The saved rows and Task selection should appear under **Updating…**, followed by fresh state. If refresh fails, the saved content should remain under one **Couldn't update** message.","files":[],"additions":1511,"deletions":116,"changed_files":23,"merge_commit":"dd78ac59dcaae446cfb62ec3e7d2614e40d177f0"},{"number":1426,"title":"release: v0.13.1","body":"# v0.13.1\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.13.1 makes conversations easier to continue and delivery failures easier to recover. Claude and Codex conversations use their native homes by default, Desktop keeps unfinished interactive work visible, and New Session can capture ideas as Tasks without disturbing existing work. Scheduled releases now retain the evidence needed to distinguish verified publication from deferred or failed attempts.\n\n## Continue conversations across Loopflow and native CLIs\n\nConversations no longer need separate account homes by default. Loopflow shares Claude and Codex history with their ordinary CLIs while retaining explicit isolation and the original home mode when resuming existing work.\n\n- Switch the shared login with `lf account <provider> use <email>`, preserving refreshed and previously unknown credentials.\n- Choose `--isolate` or `--shared`, or set the `isolate` configuration default. Existing account-pinned conversations remain isolated.\n- Use native conversation IDs in `lf session` commands; connecting imports previously untracked conversations.\n- Close owned Codex engines when their driver exits while preserving saved history, live takeovers, and unrelated conversations.\n\n## Find unfinished work and capture the next idea\n\nOrdinary Desktop and CLI Session lists now focus on unfinished interactive conversations and current authored reviews. New Session adds a repository-local skill picker so exploring an idea can lead to Tasks while existing work stays in place.\n\n- Choose a skill beside New Session. The default is `capture-tasks`, and the choice is remembered per repository; selecting a skill does not launch it.\n- Launch from the repository sidebar or a Wave context menu. A selected Task supplies its parent Wave as context while Task selection and existing terminal layouts are preserved.\n- Use `capture-tasks` to explore ownership across Waves and repositories and file self-contained Tasks without starting workers or binding the capture conversation. The guidance preserves successful filings and reconciles uncertain writes before retrying.\n- Keep navigation counts aligned with the filtered Session list. Filtering preserves selection, prepared commands, drafts, and retained native surfaces; a finished turn does not explicitly complete a Session.\n- Enable Show headless Sessions or use `lf session list --interactive all --history` for full inspection. `--needs-me` narrows the selected mode.\n\n## Recover launches and delivery without losing evidence\n\nThis patch addresses failures where one stalled operation could block unrelated work or hide a retained delivery. Release accounting now follows each scheduled opportunity through execution, verification, and its product outcome.\n\n- Prevent stalled native dispatch from holding SQLite across provider I/O. Session-scoped locking retains stale-driver rejection while allowing unrelated database writes.\n- Confirm Linear creation, edits, and completion against the exact issue and its Project association, avoiding a full-Wave refresh as a prerequisite for local preparation and retaining duplicate-safe retries.\n- Preserve pending PR deliveries when retiring obsolete Home supervision, including delivery intent, failures, CI state, and terminal history.\n- Start detached workers in their requested checkout even when the tmux server was left in a deleted directory. Spawn errors identify the requested directory.\n- Inspect verified publication, verified no-change, deferred work, and failures with `lf release history`; record repair ownership through `lf cron disposition`.\n- Catch up missed release times with one execution per wake, retaining original timing and unfinished candidates. Multiple missed opportunities covered by one execution do not become multiple successful releases.\n- Recover interrupted release checks using process and lock evidence. Publication settlement requires the exact candidate, public artifact read-back, and installer smoke checks; retained artifacts support retries of missing publication stages.\n\n## Operational not","files":[],"additions":97,"deletions":60,"changed_files":8,"merge_commit":"49f8385f0f41194434ce208a2b63ea10b570e723"},{"number":1427,"title":"Speed up lf wt list and report real invocation timings","body":"Listing worktrees could take several seconds in a busy repository, with database contention adding further delays. This PR reduces repeated Git work, fixes a Codex dispatch deadlock, and adds timing reports to explain remaining waits.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Make lf wt list fast under real worktree load · LOO-375](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-375/make-lf-wt-list-fast-under-real-worktree-load)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Batch branch reads, run local checks concurrently, and cache squash-merge and diff-stat results by commit pair.\n- Overlap remote enrichment with local checks and combine GitHub PR state and branch existence into one request. Remote commands have a 10-second timeout; a GitHub timeout skips the fallback and leaves PR state unknown.\n- Share one 15-second SQLite wait budget across Exec receipts. Give Codex dispatch an independent deadline and move blocking history writes off runtime workers.\n- Add `lf wt timing [--json]` with counts, median/p95 durations, phase timings, and failures. Samples live in a bounded file outside SQLite so database contention does not prevent recording them.\n- Let completed Sessions with confirmed-dead providers release Task gates; live, unknown, and unfinished Sessions still block. Worktree deletion now reads the plain worktree list without computing unrelated status.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded benchmarks on a busy host with 51 worktrees reduced median text listing time from 12.83 s to 1.92 s and JSON from 10.47 s to 1.63 s. Text and JSON output were byte-identical on the measured repository state. Method and limitations are preserved in `scripts/benchmarks/wt-list/README.md`.\n\nThe ≤1 s online p95 target remains unmet: measured p95 was 2.36 s for text and 1.93 s for JSON. Ordinary-use timing and quiet-host measurements remain outstanding.\n\nRecorded gate results: formatting, Clippy, architecture checks, 56 focused Rust tests, 333 Python tests, and 78 website tests passed (3 skipped). The full Rust matrix was deferred to CI after the local resource preflight failed; worktree integration and CLI binary tests were not rerun at the final tree. Checks were not rerun for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: run `lf wt list` several times, then `lf wt timing`. The listing should retain its familiar output, while the report shows sample counts, median/p95 latency, and time spent in startup, local Git, remote requests, and Exec receipts.","files":[],"additions":2196,"deletions":399,"changed_files":27,"merge_commit":"7a6ae0d7094f97ca5a8895e21abc466e557e7ab5"},{"number":1428,"title":"Remove permission conditions from builtin agent instructions","body":"Loopflow's builtin instructions repeatedly told agents to seek permission for ordinary work. Jack Heart requested removing that framing: ownership supplies context and tracking, while the task and its review steps determine what happens next.\n\n## What changes\n\nRemove the push-permission rule, the repository and Wave Session Limits sections, and permission conditions throughout builtin skills and related docs. Describe each operation directly while retaining review feedback, task scope, preservation of active work, and evidence requirements. Preserve technical OAuth, harness, filesystem and process-control terminology, along with dated historical records and the explicit guidance not to ask permission for reversible work.\n\nRegenerate prompt goldens and update the headless-prompt assertion. No Rust runtime behavior changes. Based on main after #1412.\n\n## Checks\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --lib -- engine::builtins engine::prompt` — 89 passed.\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --test golden_prompt` — passed.\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --test documented_commands` — 3 passed.\n- `cargo fmt --check` — passed.\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` — passed.\n- Reviewed the requested text sweep and the diff: remaining matches describe technical controls, historical decisions, or guidance against permission prompts. Review and evidence requirements remain intact.\n\nPublished for Jack Heart's review and merge.","files":[],"additions":195,"deletions":235,"changed_files":48,"merge_commit":"04105d34059d3516b9c159c874aca9a8a07daa85"},{"number":1429,"title":"Restart Tasks without stranding the previous review","body":"Restarting a Task at a waiting review previously replaced its Flow while leaving the review service and provider alive. The old review then blocked admission but could no longer be completed.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Restart Tasks without stranding the previous review · LOO-377](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-377/restart-tasks-without-stranding-the-previous-review)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\nRestart now serializes with review launch, fences the exact pending Session, records process identities before native receipt cleanup, and waits for owned execution to exit before replacement. Review capture/native history and Task/PR/checkout identities survive; retirement records no successful review. Independent reviews and unknown process ownership remain blockers.\n\nValidation: focused public restart tests passed for live owned processes, interruption after provider cleanup without a Session provider PID, launch serialization, failed-launch continuation with the same replacement, independent reviews and unknown ownership. The store retirement/fencing/rollback test passed. Architecture and formatting passed. The full local gate stopped before product suites at its disk reserve (13.3 GiB available; 32 GiB required), so hosted CI owns Clippy, the full suites and the additional direct launch-lock regression.\n\nJack Heart authorized autonomous publication and landing without interactive review. LOO-370's implementation and processes were untouched. Source and merge evidence do not establish installed acceptance. The existing idle managed Flow is preserved; no step completion is fabricated.\n\nCI repair: the first full Rust run passed 2,210 tests and failed two fixture setups. The stale-review replacement fixture now retires its previous review; the launch-lock fixture starts an unclaimed review instead of requesting a worker claim. Hosted CI must verify the repaired head.","files":[],"additions":937,"deletions":98,"changed_files":16,"merge_commit":"c5dc238b0afb53dbe7098f30e2dee8085b53e1e3"},{"number":1430,"title":"Resume the current worktree’s last conversation with lf resume","body":"Returning to a conversation required finding its ID. `lf resume` now selects the current worktree’s interactive Session by its latest human message, so assistant output and background work do not change which conversation opens.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf session resume [ID]` and its shorthand, `lf resume [ID]`. Explicit IDs accept Loopflow Sessions and native Claude or Codex conversations, including another worktree; the provider opens in the selected Session’s recorded directory.\n- Match physical worktree roots, including subdirectories and symlinks, while excluding sibling worktrees, headless Sessions, and stale Flow reviews.\n- Read Claude and Codex input timestamps from the recorded provider home. When unavailable, fall back per Session to its last interactive opening, then creation time.\n- Allow completed conversations to reopen when saved native history is available. Flow progression remains `lf flow resume`.\n\nAutomatic selection covers Sessions already recorded by Loopflow; explicit provider IDs can connect native conversations for the first time. Opening receipts record successful process or IDE handoff, not confirmed UI readiness.\n\n## Checks\n\n`lf queue -b` completed compress → sync → realign → gate. No production changes were needed.\n\n- Final gate: 139 affected tests passed under network isolation, covering Session CLI/lifecycle, PR and review continuation, documented commands, Session selection, opening receipts, navigation, and Flow graph behavior. Inherited Loopflow authority was cleared and disposable provider homes used.\n- Formatting, Clippy, and architecture checks passed.\n- Earlier broad library run: 1,690 passed, 4 failed, 8 ignored. Those four passed in isolated reruns; one reported leaked handles. Final gate covers the affected subset, not the full library or CI matrix.\n- Full matrix remains CI-owned.\n\n## Demo and remaining coverage\n\nJack Heart confirmed the default Codex terminal resume demo worked on 2026-10-04 and approved the PR. The source-build demo used a disposable Loopflow Home with existing conversations pinned to their provider account directory. The installed CLI has not been replaced.\n\nThe full multi-conversation scenario remains a suggested walkthrough: open conversations A and B in one worktree, send the latest message to A, then let B produce more output. From a subdirectory, run `lf resume`; A should reopen. A newer conversation in a sibling worktree should not affect the choice.\n\nRun `lf resume <Claude-or-Codex-ID>` to reopen a specific native conversation. Claude and IDE demos, explicit cross-worktree opening, and the full A/B ranking scenario remain unverified interactively. The CLI fixture covers adjacent-directory exclusion but does not construct a second linked Git worktree.\n\nKnown limitation from source review: Claude filters injected-message markers anywhere in message text, so a real pasted message containing one can be omitted from recency. Missing input evidence falls back per Session; an older recognized message remains the input timestamp when present.","files":[],"additions":736,"deletions":15,"changed_files":10,"merge_commit":"16fa9742591e3edfc0ed42c913c64347c02ffeb5"},{"number":1431,"title":"Give pursue one implementation loop and keep reviews in code and feature","body":"Pursue now runs one implementation loop and publishes when ready. Code adds a human PR walkthrough; feature keeps design review, a separate demo, gate and landing. Existing captured Flows retain their steps.\n\nThe authoring guide and launch instructions match those boundaries. Infrastructure memory also records the completed legacy-store retirement and verified review-restart delivery.\n\nCI exposed three tests coupled to the old defaults. Controller progression now proves demo proceeds forward into gate and landing; generic nested-review and Session-recording proofs use explicit fixture Flows. Testing guidance names controller and CLI coverage alongside Flow graph checks.\n\nValidation after repair: 23 controller planning tests and 23 CLI Flow tests passed (3 existing ignored); formatting and all-target Clippy passed. Earlier checks passed 55 parser/graph tests and 10 post-conflict graph tests. Hosted checks verify the published head.\n\nInfrastructure default activation remains pending a published installation containing these definitions.","files":[],"additions":99,"deletions":140,"changed_files":12,"merge_commit":"a1d2f8a591e14f52d29b87fe56a27eccd5e31f8f"},{"number":1432,"title":"Record and report real Desktop launch and refresh timings","body":"Diagnosing slow Desktop startup previously required a separate recording or staged benchmark. Desktop now keeps local timing records from normal use, so maintainers can compare launches and identify slow reads by app version.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Open Desktop immediately from one cached workspace · LOO-376](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-376/open-desktop-immediately-from-one-cached-workspace)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 2 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Record launch milestones, cache hits, `lf` read durations and workspace refresh outcomes under `<Home>/desktop-cache/timings/`.\n- Add a text and JSON report with sample counts, medians, p95 after 20 samples, maxima and failure counts. Separate usability timings for saved and fresh workspace data.\n- Bound each journal file to 256 KB, omit command arguments and output, and disable recording in app test modes.\n- Add Swift journal and Python report coverage, and fix empty-array handling in the Desktop test script.\n\nThese measurements do not establish a startup speed improvement. First-frame timing measures a render-server commit; usability measures observed outline rows. Running or early-quit launches count as missing any milestones they have not reached.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough; a real launch has not yet been recorded for validation: open Desktop normally, then run `uv run python scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/timings.py`. The report should show launch milestones and read durations for the app version. Add `--json` for structured output.","files":[],"additions":557,"deletions":12,"changed_files":15,"merge_commit":"464ac18f0100a2fee0504e1f0684b8714e104088"},{"number":1433,"title":"Record installation and checkout closure evidence for LOO-292","body":"Infrastructure memory still described LOO-292's installation, schedule and checkout proof as outstanding. This memory-only PR records the October 4 machine evidence the Task closes on and what was not observed.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Keep published installation and main checkout updates reliable · LOO-292](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-292/keep-published-installation-and-main-checkout-updates-reliable)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 3 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Record published 0.13.0 installing itself at a real login after the laptop missed the release while off, real launchd calendar firings of the refresh job, verified receipt/app/signature integrity, a containerized fresh install, repeat and repair, and installed-binary checkout sync preserving unpublished commits and uncommitted bytes in a sandbox.\n- Name what stays unobserved: a Monday 09:00 firing, a sleep-coalesced wake run, interactive app acceptance, and two non-blocking installer observations.\n- Replace the October 2 evidence section and trim schedule statements it duplicated.\n\n## Checks\n\n`git diff --check` passes; no code changed.","files":[],"additions":40,"deletions":28,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"58d3b5b3e05135a3267d0f5340e435044440bb49"}],"decisions":null,"previous_release_notes":"# v0.13.1\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.13.1 makes conversations easier to continue and delivery failures easier to recover. Claude and Codex conversations use their native homes by default, Desktop keeps unfinished interactive work visible, and New Session can capture ideas as Tasks without disturbing existing work. Scheduled releases now retain the evidence needed to distinguish verified publication from deferred or failed attempts.\n\n## Continue conversations across Loopflow and native CLIs\n\nConversations no longer need separate account homes by default. Loopflow shares Claude and Codex history with their ordinary CLIs while retaining explicit isolation and the original home mode when resuming existing work.\n\n- Switch the shared login with `lf account <provider> use <email>`, preserving refreshed and previously unknown credentials.\n- Choose `--isolate` or `--shared`, or set the `isolate` configuration default. Existing account-pinned conversations remain isolated.\n- Use native conversation IDs in `lf session` commands; connecting imports previously untracked conversations.\n- Close owned Codex engines when their driver exits while preserving saved history, live takeovers, and unrelated conversations.\n\n## Find unfinished work and capture the next idea\n\nOrdinary Desktop and CLI Session lists now focus on unfinished interactive conversations and current authored reviews. New Session adds a repository-local skill picker so exploring an idea can lead to Tasks while existing work stays in place.\n\n- Choose a skill beside New Session. The default is `capture-tasks`, and the choice is remembered per repository; selecting a skill does not launch it.\n- Launch from the repository sidebar or a Wave context menu. A selected Task supplies its parent Wave as context while Task selection and existing terminal layouts are preserved.\n- Use `capture-tasks` to explore ownership across Waves and repositories and file self-contained Tasks without starting workers or binding the capture conversation. The guidance preserves successful filings and reconciles uncertain writes before retrying.\n- Keep navigation counts aligned with the filtered Session list. Filtering preserves selection, prepared commands, drafts, and retained native surfaces; a finished turn does not explicitly complete a Session.\n- Enable Show headless Sessions or use `lf session list --interactive all --history` for full inspection. `--needs-me` narrows the selected mode.\n\n## Recover launches and delivery without losing evidence\n\nThis patch addresses failures where one stalled operation could block unrelated work or hide a retained delivery. Release accounting now follows each scheduled opportunity through execution, verification, and its product outcome.\n\n- Prevent stalled native dispatch from holding SQLite across provider I/O. Session-scoped locking retains stale-driver rejection while allowing unrelated database writes.\n- Confirm Linear creation, edits, and completion against the exact issue and its Project association, avoiding a full-Wave refresh as a prerequisite for local preparation and retaining duplicate-safe retries.\n- Preserve pending PR deliveries when retiring obsolete Home supervision, including delivery intent, failures, CI state, and terminal history.\n- Start detached workers in their requested checkout even when the tmux server was left in a deleted directory. Spawn errors identify the requested directory.\n- Inspect verified publication, verified no-change, deferred work, and failures with `lf release history`; record repair ownership through `lf cron disposition`.\n- Catch up missed release times with one execution per wake, retaining original timing and unfinished candidates. Multiple missed opportunities covered by one execution do not become multiple successful releases.\n- Recover interrupted release checks using process and lock evidence. Publication settlement requires the exact candidate, public artifact read-back, and installer smoke checks; retained artifacts support retries of missing publication stages.\n\n## Operational notes\n\nShared account switching also changes the login used by plain Claude and Codex. Running Codex engines retain their original login until restarted; an in-flight turn may fail once, with automatic recovery for headless runs. Shared Codex switching requires file-based credential storage. Forwarded Codex login uses an experimental protocol and requires Loopflow’s engine.\n\nRelease locks and checkout leases now extend through Git, PR, hook, CI-repair, and publisher children, preventing controller exit from permitting overlapping publication or premature checkout removal. Schedule replacements on the same Home retain unfinished candidates; changing Homes leaves unresolved work available for repair without transferring execution authority.\n\nUpgrade running binaries to receive the dispatch fix. Desktop’s new skill launch requires a CLI with `capture-tasks` and explicit skill launch support. Automated regressions cover the release recovery paths, but installed acceptance, public artifact and installer proof, and repeated automatic release execution remain follow-up validation. Native picker interaction and live cross-repository Task capture also remain unverified end to end.\n\n## Small changes\n\n- Passing Dependabot updates now enter the required merge queue through a dedicated repository token, with retries after successful CI and required checks preserved.\n- Update Ruff, thiserror, tiktoken-rs, tokio-tungstenite, and dirs.\n- Generated PR reviews focus on behavior, data models, and APIs.","source_limits":{"context_bytes":131072,"commits":256,"merged_prs":200,"files_per_change":100,"area_scopes":32,"name_bytes":256,"title_bytes":300,"path_bytes":512,"pr_body_bytes":4096,"decisions_bytes":16384,"previous_release_notes_bytes":16384,"release_notes_bytes":61440},"omissions":{"commits":0,"merged_prs":0,"commit_files":27,"pr_files":0,"area_scopes":0,"text_bytes":13725,"decisions_bytes":0,"previous_release_notes_bytes":0}}